WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.477 --> 00:00:08.060
ROB SADOWSKI: My
name is Rob Sadowski.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.460
I work in the product
organization on security

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.787
and trust here at Google Cloud.

00:00:12.787 --> 00:00:14.120
We'll also have Eugene Liderman.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.040
He's going to speak
later about devices,

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.390
and I'll let him do his own
introduction in publicity.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.010
So what are we going
to cover today?

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.080
Here's our agenda.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.020
To do this topic
justice, I think

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:31.040
first we need to describe what
we mean by modern end user

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.000
computing.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.440
What's modern?

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.970
Who are the users?

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.100
This baseline
understanding is really

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.200
going to help us
understand and think

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.830
through what it takes to
provide robust enterprise

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.260
security in that environment.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.482
We're going to need to
think about applications.

00:00:47.482 --> 00:00:49.190
We're going to need
to think about users.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.590
We're going to need to
think about devices.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.080
And securing each of these
presents an unique set

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:55.440
of challenges.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.060
But solving the overall
challenge of modern end user

00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:59.810
computing and how we
make sure that that's

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.150
secure and productive
is going to require

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.790
us to follow some basic,
good security principles.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:07.910
We're going to take a
defense in-depth approach.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:11.180
We're going to have a solution
that encompasses protection

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.550
at multiple layers,
in every layer,

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.890
in a much more holistic way.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.120
So let's get in and
start with some context

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.550
about the modern end user
computing environment

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.860
because it's the thing
that's creating the security

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:29.370
challenges and the opportunities
that we're all facing today.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.310
So everyone hold up your phones.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.810
I know you were told
to silence your phones.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.915
Yes, everyone has a phone.

00:01:35.915 --> 00:01:37.790
And how many of you are
taking notes or doing

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.660
something else on a laptop?

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.130
Some people.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.140
So this is today's reality.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.670
This is what we're talking
about in terms of modern user

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:47.290
computing.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:51.280
We work wherever we are, even
if we're in a movie theater,

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.230
at a conference
in San Francisco.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.460
How is this possible?

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.660
Well, one, the access
devices support it.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.890
We have always on connectivity.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.370
We have really, much more
convenient form factors

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.980
than lugging around a desktop
or something like that.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.130
We have a decreasing
dependence on fat client apps

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.560
that have to work across
multiple operating systems

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:12.680
and things like that.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.300
And that means that you, and
your employees, and the people

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.320
that you work with--

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.340
the users on these devices can
access your work apps, not just

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:24.130
the consumer-side apps that
you're consumerizing and using

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.400
potentially for work stuff.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.770
But you can actually
access the primary tools

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.420
in apps you use to
get your job done

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.530
through a browser or a much
more lightweight mobile client.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:40.090
Second, what we are
accessing is increasingly not

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.430
sitting in one of
our own data centers.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.680
And that traditionally has
created a lot of challenges

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.440
around connectivity and
doing that at scale.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.510
So much more of what
we are accessing

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:53.530
to get our primary work
done is in the cloud.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:56.110
And therefore, it's
on a public network.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:59.170
And these networks are
something to which we

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.390
have pretty ubiquitous access.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.580
You came to this
conference and just assumed

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.070
that you'd have a pretty solid
high bandwidth connection.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:07.540
Now, that's mostly worked.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:08.980
If you were here last year--

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:09.860
didn't work so well.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.040
But this year, so
far so good, right?

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:15.022
So we all assume that we were
going to be able to do that,

00:03:15.022 --> 00:03:15.730
and that's great.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.350
But failing that-- guess what?

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:18.100
We have a backup.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.110
We have wireless
connectivity on our phones.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:22.540
So we are always connected.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.970
And that creates a
lot of opportunities,

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:28.750
but potentially,
some challenges.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:33.460
But why else have we embraced
mobility so strongly?

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.570
Because what we find
is that when we do,

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:39.040
it leads to increased
productivity,

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.380
increased collaboration,
and reduced overhead.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.980
So some of the data
here is actually really,

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:45.730
really compelling.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.310
There's been a two-year
Stanford study-- two years,

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:50.680
so it's not just, hey,
do a quick survey--

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.040
that showed a 13%
increase in productivity

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.500
when users work from home.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.990
Again, that's that
modern end user model.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.870
Other research indicates
that employee mobility

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:04.630
leads to 30% better
business processes,

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:07.720
better following of processes,
better redesigning things

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.460
to work better, and
23% more productivity.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.760
These are real
significant gains.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.590
And that we find--
and I think this

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:17.649
is our own experience--
that working from home

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:19.060
reduces attrition.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.579
So retaining and
keeping talent is

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:23.380
so important in today's world.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.140
Anything we can do and
something with a lever

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.100
like that-- so important.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.150
The other thing is that these
devices and this way of working

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.730
allows us to communicate
and work asynchronously.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.090
So asynchronous
communications that

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.240
are facilitated by devices
and cloud technology

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.013
allow us to be more efficient.

00:04:42.013 --> 00:04:43.930
Web-based meetings are
better planned and more

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.000
apt to stay on message.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.667
I can't remember the last time I
didn't have a web-based meeting

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:49.600
or do things on
conference calls.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:50.980
But more and more
and more, we're

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.480
getting more done in less time.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.380
The net net is that modern
communication and collaboration

00:04:56.380 --> 00:05:00.550
can increase productivity,
again, 25%, 30%, plus,

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.140
and increase employee
satisfaction.

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.690
And yes, usually when
we talk about benefits,

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:05.860
we go right to costs.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.350
And certainly, there's an
element of cost savings here--

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:11.290
something like that.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:12.970
You look at what
IBM does-- they've

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.640
been really public about
how they have moved

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.560
to, essentially, a
remote workforce--

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.120
folks that work and are
enabled by these technologies.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.745
And they've saved
more than $50 million

00:05:21.745 --> 00:05:24.130
by making many of
their positions remote.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.880
So while cost savings probably
isn't the primary benefit

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.410
in a lot of cases, it is
certainly part of the equation.

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:36.370
But this new model of
working means we are also

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:39.490
faced with a new set of risks.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:41.770
Security professionals--
when you say, OK,

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:43.220
what's going on now?

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:44.920
What are you concerned about?

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:47.920
They find a lot of challenges
in defending against threats

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.280
to mobile devices.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.900
And this survey ranks them the
number one hardest enterprise

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:54.100
asset to defend.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.080
There's a lot of
user intervention.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:58.233
There's a lot of
user control there.

00:05:58.233 --> 00:05:59.650
That's a pretty
strong statement--

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:03.340
the number one or most
difficult asset to defend.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:06.160
And 3/4 of organizations
report that they've

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:08.050
suffered some sort
of breach or incident

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:10.100
as a result of a
mobile security issue.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:13.020
So these aren't likely to be
the big mega breaches where

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.880
you aren't storing
hundreds of millions

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:17.390
of records on a mobile device.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:22.375
But nevertheless, when we
have regulations like GDPR,

00:06:22.375 --> 00:06:27.220
where we have to disclose
when personal information is

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.470
disclosed-- that type
of information does

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:31.190
get onto devices.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.880
And so this is an
increasing concern,

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:37.120
even if it isn't the large,
mega catastrophic breach

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:38.140
that it may be.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.570
It's all still very,
very important.

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:43.520
So how do we enable
the business?

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.700
How do we unleash
that productivity

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:50.350
without increasing or creating
unnecessary security risk?

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:52.690
Well, to do that,
we need a strategy

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:56.140
that thinks through and
understands the whole stack

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:59.750
that we are using to deliver
IT services in this new world.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.320
And as I said before, we
have to take a defense

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:03.200
in-depth approach.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:06.130
It's not just about the
device, although that's

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:07.220
really important.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.100
It starts at the infrastructure.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:10.690
We have to think
about the apps that

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:12.280
are on these
devices and the apps

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.080
that we are
delivering information

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.630
through to these workers.

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:18.370
We have to think about
the users and how

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:21.020
we establish their identity.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:23.330
We have to think, again,
about the devices.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.810
So we need to build
protection into every layer

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.660
and take advantage of the
controls that are there so we

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.310
can build end-to-end security.

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:34.150
So what I'd like to do now is
walk through what we can do

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:36.280
in each layer and
what specifically,

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:39.160
you can get from Google Cloud--
what we're doing in many

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:40.390
of these--

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:41.480
in each.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:46.090
So perhaps now, but
certainly going forward,

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:50.260
the services that workers
access to do their jobs

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.410
will be largely
based in the cloud.

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:56.110
And while this used to be
seen as a source of risk,

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:58.360
it's now seen-- cloud
infrastructure is now

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:01.360
seen as a security
enabler or a strength.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.180
Gartner says that over
the next several years,

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.470
workloads that leverage
cloud infrastructure--

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:07.820
whatever those workloads are--

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:11.920
will suffer 60% fewer incidents
than those in traditional data

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:12.640
centers.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:15.550
That is a really, really
strong compelling statement.

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:19.690
And I think that the experience
that people have in operating

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:20.940
the cloud bears this out.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.560
Another study that I cited
in another talk this week

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:28.300
is that going from last year to
this year of organizations that

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:30.700
are putting applications
in the cloud, 90%

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:33.340
plus of them trust
the cloud more.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:35.502
It's that repeated
positive experience

00:08:35.502 --> 00:08:37.419
that they are getting,
that they are realizing

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:39.640
that the cloud, actually--
cloud infrastructure--

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.600
is not part of a
security problem.

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:42.830
It's part of the solution.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:45.520
So how do we leverage
that in this modern world

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:46.930
to increase security?

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.452
So the foundational
layer of our stack--

00:08:49.452 --> 00:08:51.910
that's what I want you to take
away-- the infrastructure is

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:54.340
getting more secure over time.

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:56.260
In a Google Cloud, the
infrastructure layer

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:59.050
that we provide offers
many unique benefits

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:00.590
that you can take advantage of.

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:04.720
So we do a lot of security by
default. When data is stored--

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.570
our services that run in
our cloud when you put data

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:08.260
in there--

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:14.350
all of that data is
encrypted at rest by default,

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.840
without any interaction
required by you

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:19.670
on behalf of the customer
or you as a customer.

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:21.670
The second thing that
we do or second thing

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.670
I want to highlight is that
the worst thing that can happen

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:28.210
is that someone is working
remotely or working

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:31.210
in the field, and they can't
get to the application.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:33.760
The size and scope
of our network,

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:36.790
as well as the protections
we've built in by default,

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:40.780
help organizations mitigate
potential network and DDoS

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.525
attacks really, really well.

00:09:42.525 --> 00:09:43.900
The other thing
is that as you're

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:48.160
deploying apps for those
users to work on, again,

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:51.400
downtime really impacts them.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:54.160
We do all the infrastructure
patching automatically.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:56.965
So not only do you
get the benefits

00:09:56.965 --> 00:09:58.840
of a constantly updated
infrastructure that's

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:00.880
not subject to
vulnerabilities, but we

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:02.380
don't have to take downtime.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:06.730
And this was a
particularly great feature

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:08.783
when the processor
vulnerabilities

00:10:08.783 --> 00:10:11.200
or a Spectre and Meltdown that
Google researchers actually

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.143
found needed to be fixed.

00:10:13.143 --> 00:10:14.560
So across our whole
fleet, we were

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:16.960
absolutely able to patch
that in Google Cloud

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:18.760
without taking any
downtime, which

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:21.080
is not the case on
many other services.

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:22.990
The second thing is
that we are building on

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:26.720
or you are building on a
purpose-built infrastructure.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:29.530
So we design all the
way from the chip

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:34.113
up to the chiller in the data
center, our own infrastructure.

00:10:34.113 --> 00:10:36.280
That's the chips that go
in the servers, the servers

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:40.300
themselves, the storage, the
networking gear-- all of that.

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:42.010
We have a private
global network.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:44.710
And so when we are
accessing services on that,

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:47.620
traffic that gets off
the public internet

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:49.660
and gets on that
network is much less

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:52.893
likely to be intercepted
or manipulated.

00:10:52.893 --> 00:10:55.060
And again, I talked a little
bit about the chip side

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:56.768
of things-- we have
hardware-based tamper

00:10:56.768 --> 00:10:59.910
protection to make sure that the
infrastructure that your apps,

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:04.540
services, and data sits on is
always in a state that we know.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:07.700
That we know exactly what is
running there at all times.

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:10.000
And then-- we'll get into
this a little bit later on--

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.000
there's a lot of proprietary
controls and expertise

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.370
that are embedded here that
you can take advantage of.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:18.340
So we'll talk a little
bit about the way

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:21.970
that this is G Suite,
like Gmail, do things

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:25.450
like phishing and protection
against malicious email.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:28.570
We talk about the easy
way you can implement

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:31.090
strong authentication with
security keys and some

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:32.410
of the things we do there.

00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:35.560
We talk about the ways we
can prevent data exfiltration

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:37.030
from certain
services, with things,

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:39.570
like, VPC Service Controls.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:41.550
And just this week,
we introduced a set

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.580
of what we call user protection
services that help deal

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:48.880
with risk of suspicious
or malicious URLs

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:52.200
and applications that
look at, again, other ways

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.830
to do phishing defense
and protect applications

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:59.350
against abuse by bots
or other like that.

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:02.160
And the other thing that we
do from a security perspective

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.538
that is really important as
the operator or administrator

00:12:05.538 --> 00:12:07.080
of the infrastructure
like this is we

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:10.420
provide a tremendous amount of
visibility and transparency.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:13.050
So you maybe heard Urs
in the keynote today talk

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:16.050
about access
transparency, where you

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:18.750
can understand if any cloud
administrators are ever

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.450
accessing your data
in the course of doing

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:22.660
support and things like that.

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:27.210
So you can feel confident that
what you are doing is robust.

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:31.290
We also give a lot of visibility
into just overall operations

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:34.800
with our Cloud Security
Command Center on GCP

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:37.320
and our Security
Center within G Suite

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:40.200
to really highlight where
are the particular areas

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:41.670
of security risk.

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:44.010
So what I want to,
again, to take away

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:46.290
is that there's a
really robust foundation

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.540
for building the
apps and delivering

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:54.090
the services that these modern
end users, the mobile users,

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:55.233
need.

00:12:55.233 --> 00:12:57.900
The other thing is that building
on an infrastructure like this,

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.670
you get the assurance that
not only the infrastructure

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:03.630
but the services that sit on
top are validated against some

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:06.930
of the most rigorous global
security and privacy standards

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:10.520
like the SO 27000 series.

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:14.110
And we know that as you adopt
this infrastructure foundation,

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:16.960
you may want to bring some of
your existing security tools

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:17.650
with you.

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:21.250
So we have an extensive
ecosystem of security partners

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:26.020
that deliver their capabilities
on our cloud platform that

00:13:26.020 --> 00:13:29.170
runs the gamut, from things like
identity and access management

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:32.810
solutions, monitoring solutions,
GRC solutions, Endpoint

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:34.060
Security--

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:37.420
which I'll talk about
in a few slides here.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:41.110
Cloud Access Security brokers,
which are really important

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:44.980
for some organizations in
getting the level of control

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:46.930
that they want in this
[INAUDIBLE] world.

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:49.630
So you can preserve
existing investment things

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:52.300
like your endpoint
protection platforms

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:54.520
and still leverage
those as you build

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:56.570
on our cloud infrastructure.

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:59.590
So hopefully, you
take away why cloud--

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:01.870
that foundation
in many ways does

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:04.790
or will underpin what we're
doing around modern end user

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:05.290
computing.

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:07.180
So now, let's turn
our attention to some

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:10.090
of the layers of the stack
we more commonly associate

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:13.990
with these challenges about
the apps, about the users,

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:15.770
and about the devices.

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:17.500
So the layer above
the infrastructure

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:18.970
is the application
layer, and these

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:22.570
can be applications that you
build or deploy in the cloud

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:23.560
yourself.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:25.990
Or they can be SaaS
apps or other services

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:26.830
from a third-party.

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:29.372
But regardless, we need to think
about, what are the features

00:14:29.372 --> 00:14:31.790
and what our capabilities
that we're looking for?

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:35.140
So in terms of what we offer
and the way we think about some

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:37.300
of this within
Google Cloud, G Suite

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:39.580
offers many security
benefits that

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:42.145
are really attuned to the
needs of this modern workforce.

00:14:42.145 --> 00:14:45.250
They're much more collaborative
and things like that.

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:46.890
First, G Suite is very--

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:48.940
we have these core
guiding principles

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:52.090
around being proactive, about
being intelligent, making

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:54.670
things simple, and
building compliance.

00:14:54.670 --> 00:14:56.830
So in terms of being
proactive, what

00:14:56.830 --> 00:14:58.780
we are looking to
do is automatically

00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:02.600
block many of the threats
that target cloud workers,

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.030
especially in email.

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:06.430
I think we're all aware
that email is the number one

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:10.120
app, the most critical
app for these mobile, more

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:11.030
modern workers.

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:16.930
And G Suite has 99.9% plus
accuracy in blocking spam,

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:19.960
in blocking malicious email,
in dealing with business email

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:21.790
compromise type scams.

00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:25.130
All of the outbound email
is checked automatically,

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:28.210
if you turn on things like
DLP for sensitive information,

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:30.800
and so that can be
automatically flagged-- again,

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:35.030
potentially eliminating sources
of risk or data exposure.

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:37.000
And there are built-in
defenses as email

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.760
comes in against attachments
carrying ransomware

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:44.110
and other malware that
can get on the device

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:49.120
that users are using and affect
effective security there.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:51.040
And today, we announced
some new advances

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:54.910
in G Suite in this
area, around stronger

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:57.310
phishing and malware protection.

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:59.350
And some sandboxes
technology that

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:02.230
goes even further in
allowing us to deal with more

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:03.550
sophisticated malware.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:07.270
So we constantly keep
pushing and trying to advance

00:16:07.270 --> 00:16:08.452
the state of the art.

00:16:08.452 --> 00:16:09.910
The other thing
that we think about

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:11.680
is, again, this
notion of intelligence

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:13.040
and being proactive.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:16.630
We want to help stop
threats to this workforce,

00:16:16.630 --> 00:16:19.300
to these devices, before they
turn into damaging incidents.

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.220
And the intelligence
that's in G Suite

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:23.920
can help surface the
most worrisome security

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:26.620
concerns, like, potential
device compromises.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:29.140
Part of our G Suite
Security Center

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:31.480
has a whole specific
section that

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:34.480
allows you to look at
your device footprint

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:37.630
and understand-- is there a
potential compromise there?

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:40.210
Which users is it affecting
so you can deal with this?

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:42.010
And we try to do this
without sending you

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:45.220
constant notifications
about less important issues

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:46.690
that are less impactful.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:48.800
And again, we strive
to make these simple.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:51.790
We have this philosophy
that security tools

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:53.620
are only useful if
you actually use them,

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:55.630
if you actually deploy them.

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:58.332
And so if they're too
difficult or too complicated,

00:16:58.332 --> 00:16:59.290
they're of limited use.

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:00.880
We're always trying
to give admins

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:04.119
simpler, more streamlined
ways to deploy this.

00:17:04.119 --> 00:17:06.849
Make a lot of the things
available, again, by default--

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:08.240
like I talked about--

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:11.109
and make them work
out of the box

00:17:11.109 --> 00:17:15.490
with the right configurations
right at your fingertips.

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:18.369
Now, when I mention an
application security

00:17:18.369 --> 00:17:23.630
layer, how many people actually
thought about browsers?

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:26.760
Maybe some of you, but
perhaps, not many of you.

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:30.920
The fact is many, if not the
majority of corporate apps,

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:34.040
are or will be delivered
in browsers, even

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:35.180
up to productivity things.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:36.710
Like, think of what
we do with G Suite.

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:37.730
Think of the sophistication.

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:39.680
We get in Docs, and
Sheets, and other things

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:43.070
like that or some of the
other productivity suites.

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:45.740
It's an essential--
the browser really

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:48.740
is an essential part of
that application layer

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:52.250
that we need to have
confidence in the security of.

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.440
And Chrome Browser
for Enterprise

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:58.160
designs in many
security features.

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:00.740
We think of that in
three broad areas.

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:03.940
We try to make it safe as
much as possible by design,

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:05.960
so we build those
protections in.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:08.300
Here, again-- just like we're
describing a multilayered

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:08.720
approach--

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:10.178
Chrome takes that
same multilayered

00:18:10.178 --> 00:18:11.630
approach in the browser itself.

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:13.190
And we try to do
many, many things

00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:17.180
to keep ahead of the threat
landscape that targets the end

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:18.240
user environment.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:20.630
So in terms of being
safe by design,

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:23.660
we employ technologies
like Safe Browsing, which

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:25.970
protects from a range
of malware, phishing,

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:28.190
and social engineering
attacks-- the sites that

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:31.100
might be able to steal
passwords or infect machines

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:32.690
that would affect those users.

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:35.390
This is a constantly
updated database

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:37.730
as we crawl the web
of what we perceive

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.190
or what we see as
potentially malicious sites.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:41.690
And we'll provide
warnings to users

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:43.060
before they navigate to those.

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:45.830
So again, it gets that
distributed notion

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:50.390
of warning or control out to
users wherever they may be.

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:53.000
Chrome automatically
updates-- this

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:55.773
is so important for this
modern mobile workforce.

00:18:55.773 --> 00:18:58.190
If you're not in the office,
if you're not doing updates--

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:02.360
if IT staff and team and desktop
support staff is not touching

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:05.510
that on a regular basis
where they can do updates--

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:11.915
we have to constantly update the
OS, the application software,

00:19:11.915 --> 00:19:13.040
and all those other pieces.

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:14.810
Because we know
that vulnerabilities

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:16.670
get exploited very rapidly.

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:19.850
The fact that cloud-based
services automatically update

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:23.870
and Chrome browser especially
automatically updates

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:27.380
is really, particularly
important to make sure

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:30.410
that the users have the latest
security features and fixes

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:33.600
without requiring
any intervention.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:36.860
Sandboxing is another
thing in terms of design

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:41.570
that is very, very useful in
terms of the Chrome Security

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:42.170
model.

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:45.590
It helps prevent malware from
installing itself on devices,

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:48.440
reduces the severity and impact
of certain vulnerabilities

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:50.450
that might be exploited.

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:54.860
It also works, again,
to prevent eavesdropping

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:57.800
or other intercept
of communications

00:19:57.800 --> 00:19:59.930
as the browser
accesses websites.

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:02.630
So it uses network
security features

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:08.990
like strict transport security,
things like public key pinning,

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:12.130
things like verification
of route CAs to make sure.

00:20:12.130 --> 00:20:15.530
So building on what we do
at Google around certificate

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:19.190
transparency in doing things
like mixed script locking.

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:22.550
So there's a lot of things
or a lot of potential ways

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:25.760
that a browser can be exploited,
but the design of Chrome

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:29.390
tries to neutralize or make
many of those, much less--

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:31.310
or make itself much
less susceptible

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:33.350
to those type of techniques.

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:37.040
And Chrome really works
to stay ahead of threats.

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:39.230
There's a public
commitment to releasing

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:43.610
a fix for any critical security
vulnerability in under 60 days,

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:45.410
and on average those
vulnerabilities

00:20:45.410 --> 00:20:47.690
are fixed within 30 days.

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:51.650
Even when necessary-- we think
something is particularly

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:52.550
severe--

00:20:52.550 --> 00:20:55.530
we've pushed fixes
in under 24 hours.

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:57.860
So the team is really
always actively

00:20:57.860 --> 00:21:00.230
working to find and
fix vulnerabilities

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:02.630
before they affect
users, and that's

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:05.330
that point about trying
to stay and working

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:08.880
to stay one step ahead
of potential adversaries.

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:11.100
So that's the app layer.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:12.320
But what about users?

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:14.210
How should we think
about protecting

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:17.500
this broad and increasingly
growing user community?

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.640
I think that's the first
point I want to make.

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:24.590
The notion of who is a user
in this more modern world

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:25.680
is evolving.

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:27.740
It's not just employees.

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:28.870
It goes beyond that.

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:29.920
It's vendors.

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:31.020
It's partners.

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:32.180
It's contractors.

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:33.440
It's customers.

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:36.980
And all of those different
user constituencies

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:40.100
require different
levels of access

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:43.250
to different information,
to different data sources,

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:46.670
to different applications,
from different places,

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:47.770
on different devices.

00:21:47.770 --> 00:21:49.640
Think of that matrix.

00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:50.870
It's complicated.

00:21:50.870 --> 00:21:54.720
How can we help deal with
some of those challenges?

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:58.700
So back in 2011, Google
came up with a new approach

00:21:58.700 --> 00:22:02.120
for access management dealing
with these types of challenges.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:05.030
We called it BeyondCorp, and
this enterprise security model

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:07.670
started off as an
internal Google initiative

00:22:07.670 --> 00:22:11.570
to say that, hey, we want to
enable any of our employees

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:15.770
to work anywhere,
essentially, in the world

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:17.360
from untrusted networks.

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:19.010
We want to rely on
the internet, not

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:23.850
have to rely on a VPN,
which can be very complex.

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:26.370
This actually sounds really
close to this description

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:28.710
of modern end-user computing
that I talked about

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:29.710
at the beginning.

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:33.270
So what BeyondCorp aims to do
is shift the access controls

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:36.750
being solely based
at the network layer

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:40.140
and around the perimeter,
to switch that much, much

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:42.930
more into--

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:45.420
make it much, much
more about context--

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:47.430
about the user,
about the device,

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:50.870
and what they're accessing, and
arbit decisions based on that.

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:54.330
Don't just make an assumption
about trusting the network,

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:57.840
because we know that these
modern end-users are not

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:01.410
going to be working from
inside the office where we

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:05.170
have that presumption of trust.

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:07.650
And because of the trends that
we've been speaking about,

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:10.800
there's been tremendous
support for an idea like this.

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:13.380
If any of you were
at the RSA security

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:16.410
conference over the past month,
I think the biggest theme that

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:19.890
came out for me was
this idea around,

00:23:19.890 --> 00:23:22.140
how do we implement
zero trust networking?

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:24.000
How do we take models
like BeyondCorp

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:25.620
and actually make them real?

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.880
Because, conceptually, they
address a lot of the challenges

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:30.130
that we've been talking about.

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:32.460
Here's a great quote
from Steve Pugh.

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:34.930
He's at a company called Ionic
now, who's a Google partner,

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:38.850
but he used to be in
the office of the CTO

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.052
and various branches
of the military

00:23:41.052 --> 00:23:42.010
and in the White House.

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:44.430
And he says this
vision is really

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:46.770
the future of enterprise IT.

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:49.530
This is the modern world
that we are dealing with.

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:51.785
It's a model that
builds on zero trust

00:23:51.785 --> 00:23:54.160
and combined with best-of-breed
ideas from the community.

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:56.520
So we've tried to
adapt and see what

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:59.490
are the challenges in
terms of implementation

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:01.540
and adjust and go on.

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:04.170
And so, at Google
Cloud, we've been

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:07.620
working really hard
over the past year or so

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:11.490
to help you implement a
similar type model-- implement

00:24:11.490 --> 00:24:13.620
this BeyondCorp model
for your users--

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:17.800
through a set of products and
capabilities on our platform

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:19.140
and in our services.

00:24:19.140 --> 00:24:20.850
We put this under
the umbrella of what

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:22.800
we call context-aware access.

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:26.070
We want to enable you to
make context-aware access

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:30.090
decisions for any type of
user to any type of resource

00:24:30.090 --> 00:24:31.170
within Cloud.

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:34.680
What we do here is, we take
inputs from the user side,

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:37.320
and we take input
from the device side,

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:41.190
and then we incorporate a whole
range of contextual factors--

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:46.320
like IP address, like
location, like the time of day,

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:48.360
like the length of the session.

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:50.820
We run those through
a rules engine

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:53.220
that we call Access
Context Manager,

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:55.860
and we leverage a number
of enforcement points

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:59.400
so that we can control
access for remote users

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:01.950
to the diverse set of
resources that they're

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:03.640
looking to get access to.

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:06.390
So whether those are
web apps, whether those

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:09.540
are virtual machines, whether
those are other services

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:13.500
or resources on GCP,
whether those are APIs,

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:17.940
whether that's G Suite, whether
that's generic SaaS apps,

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:22.590
any of those we want to be able
to set precise access policies

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:25.950
and rules that take into
account the environment

00:25:25.950 --> 00:25:28.020
that users are
finding themselves in.

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:31.980
And a key enabler for this
that Google Cloud is providing

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:34.290
is our Cloud Identity solution.

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:36.840
So it's our repository
for user information

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:38.430
that we have to understand.

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:41.460
It's our identity as a service
solution or IDaaS solution

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:44.220
that offers [AUDIO OUT]
one single place to manage

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:47.170
users, apps, and devices.

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:50.250
It allows you to get this
context-aware access capability

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:52.260
for G Suite and SaaS apps.

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:56.610
It allows you to help
with the account hijacking

00:25:56.610 --> 00:25:59.400
side of things, to
implement strong two-factor

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:01.860
authentication with security
keys and other methods

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:04.020
to prevent account takeovers.

00:26:04.020 --> 00:26:08.340
It does a lot of account
protection in the background

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:10.590
based on Google's
intelligence and signals

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:12.990
and what we do to protect
the billions of accounts

00:26:12.990 --> 00:26:14.640
that access our services.

00:26:14.640 --> 00:26:17.580
It offers-- this is really
important-- integrated

00:26:17.580 --> 00:26:19.290
unified endpoint
management, both

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:22.320
from a lightweight perspective
and also more in-depth,

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:23.310
if you want to do that.

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:27.180
So to get that touch,
it offers a dashboard

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:29.250
that I made a little
bit of mention

00:26:29.250 --> 00:26:31.590
of in terms of highlighting
where are the biggest

00:26:31.590 --> 00:26:34.770
sources of risk
and what's going on

00:26:34.770 --> 00:26:37.680
across these
different identities.

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:41.970
And this is a service that is
now today used by customers

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:46.080
to manage more than 100
million users and over 55

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:47.580
million devices.

00:26:47.580 --> 00:26:51.840
So this is one of the best-kept
secrets inside Google Cloud

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:54.270
and really is a
fundamental underpinning

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:57.780
of how we are enabling and how
we are helping people to work

00:26:57.780 --> 00:26:59.650
in this new modern environment.

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:04.140
So what I wanted to do is kind
of stop talking and do a little

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:06.600
bit of a demo to show you
how this actually might work,

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:08.730
to show you how these
different layers--

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:11.790
from the infrastructure layer
to the app layer to the user

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:12.870
protection layer--

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:14.910
how all these can
work in concert

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:18.690
and how implementing
context to our access

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:21.300
for your modern end-users
actually looks like.

00:27:21.300 --> 00:27:25.140
Here's a business scenario that
poses a lot of the challenges

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:29.010
that we face dealing with
this modern end-user computing

00:27:29.010 --> 00:27:30.030
environment.

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:32.010
We have a company called Zotkix.

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:33.900
They work with a
company called Acme.

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:37.350
So already we see the complexity
and the collaborative nature

00:27:37.350 --> 00:27:38.730
of work.

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:41.550
In this scenario, the
Zotkix HR department

00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:44.880
is going to hire or bring on
some contractors from Acme

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:46.510
to help with a few
things, so they

00:27:46.510 --> 00:27:49.890
are going to need access
to the internal HR system.

00:27:49.890 --> 00:27:52.740
Well, there is no notion
of an internal system.

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:55.900
We want to make that
available as a service,

00:27:55.900 --> 00:27:58.560
so that users can
access it theoretically

00:27:58.560 --> 00:28:00.120
from wherever they are.

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:04.560
But the security policies
that they have in place

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:07.890
require some very specific
things for contractors

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:09.090
to access them.

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:12.460
They should use strong
authentication in every case.

00:28:12.460 --> 00:28:14.405
And in this particular
case for HR,

00:28:14.405 --> 00:28:15.780
we actually aren't
going to allow

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:17.280
you to work from anywhere.

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:21.690
You must be accessing this
from a company office.

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:23.430
And so the IT
department-- while this

00:28:23.430 --> 00:28:25.800
may have been a daunting
challenge in the past,

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:27.360
it's absolutely
the type of thing

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:30.550
we have to do in today's
world, in today's environment.

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:32.310
So their IT department
says consider it

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:34.780
done to both HR and security.

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:36.560
So how are they
going to do that?

00:28:36.560 --> 00:28:40.020
Well, they're going to
deploy this HR application

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:41.250
on Google Cloud.

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:44.103
They're going to define VPC-- a
Virtual Private Cloud network--

00:28:44.103 --> 00:28:46.020
for the wrap, and they're
going to stand it up

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:47.730
on Google Compute Engine.

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:50.880
And then they're going to set
up Cloud Load Balancing right

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:54.360
at the edge for ingress
from the internet,

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:56.640
and they're going to use
the Cloud Identity Aware

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:00.570
Proxy, which is integrated
right into Cloud Load Balancing,

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:02.250
to front-end the app.

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:04.280
Those are all the
features that they have.

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:06.590
They're taking advantage
of what's there.

00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:09.785
And the Identity
Aware Proxy IP is

00:29:09.785 --> 00:29:11.160
going to draw on
user information

00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:13.350
that they maintain
in Cloud Identity.

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:14.910
It's going to rely
on device status

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:17.720
from endpoint verification
and Chrome browser,

00:29:17.720 --> 00:29:19.650
and the Access
Context Manager is

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:21.640
going to be used to define
their access policy.

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:24.720
So this is why I talked about
the Cloud infrastructure layer

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:27.540
first in talking about
modern end-user computing.

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:30.090
These key enabling
services that we

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:33.590
need to enable us to
work the way we want

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:36.300
are part of the infrastructure
where the app and the data

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:37.970
and the mobile users
that they're going

00:29:37.970 --> 00:29:40.285
to access where all this lives.

00:29:40.285 --> 00:29:42.160
So let's take a look at
what this looks like.

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:43.710
The first thing
I'm going to do is

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:47.190
I'm going to set up the access
policy that restricts access

00:29:47.190 --> 00:29:49.683
to the office for this
app for contractors.

00:29:49.683 --> 00:29:51.600
And because I fear the
demagogues a little bit

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:53.580
in a movie theater and
I respect your time,

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:55.720
I actually recorded
this earlier.

00:29:55.720 --> 00:30:00.020
So what I'm going to do
is go to the GCP console,

00:30:00.020 --> 00:30:02.520
and I'm going to go to Security
and go to Access Context

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:03.270
Manager--

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:06.690
so really easy to do
right here in the console.

00:30:06.690 --> 00:30:08.820
And you can see
as we select this,

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:11.010
I have a whole set of
existing policies here,

00:30:11.010 --> 00:30:13.620
but I want to go and
define a new access level,

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:15.870
and I'm going to call
this From Acme Office.

00:30:15.870 --> 00:30:18.090
Make it very descriptive.

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:19.410
So type that in.

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:20.760
Don't always type that fast.

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:22.410
And then, I'm going to
add an attribute in here.

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:23.785
What I'm going to
do is I'm going

00:30:23.785 --> 00:30:25.470
to specify an
IT-based attribute,

00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:29.070
and I'm going to specify the
IP range that corresponds

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:30.570
to the internal office.

00:30:30.570 --> 00:30:32.740
Now, when I go ahead
and save that really,

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:36.750
really quickly, that is going
to show up in my list over here.

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:38.580
It says that my
level is created,

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:41.790
and down here I can see that new
policy is in my list of Access

00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:43.480
Context Manager policies.

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:43.980
All right.

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:45.860
So far, so good.

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:48.140
So next, I'm going
to go in and I'm

00:30:48.140 --> 00:30:51.230
going to configure the
Identity Aware Proxy.

00:30:51.230 --> 00:30:53.792
So I'm going to go in, I'm
going to pick my project.

00:30:53.792 --> 00:30:55.250
I'm going to click
on the service--

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:58.400
the back-end service that
connects to the HR app--

00:30:58.400 --> 00:30:59.650
and then, I'm going to go in.

00:30:59.650 --> 00:31:01.310
I'm going to add
members, which allows

00:31:01.310 --> 00:31:03.960
me to say what group of
users that this policy is

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:04.710
going to apply to.

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:06.530
Remember, we're identity-aware.

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:08.190
That's the key point of this.

00:31:08.190 --> 00:31:10.190
And so I'm going to add
in the group name, which

00:31:10.190 --> 00:31:11.840
includes all the contractors.

00:31:11.840 --> 00:31:14.720
I'm going to select the
policy that we just defined.

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:15.550
There it is.

00:31:15.550 --> 00:31:18.720
Scroll down from
the Acme Office.

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:20.960
And I'm going to
save all of this.

00:31:20.960 --> 00:31:22.460
It's going to instantiate.

00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:24.770
It's going to get loaded
along with the other policies

00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:26.450
that you see there on the right.

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:27.950
And so, that's the
admin experience.

00:31:27.950 --> 00:31:29.900
It's really straightforward.

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:32.000
So this might have seemed--

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:33.290
wow, this is really complex.

00:31:33.290 --> 00:31:35.580
But it's actually fairly
simple to step through.

00:31:35.580 --> 00:31:36.080
All right.

00:31:36.080 --> 00:31:39.430
Now let's flip it over and take
a look at the user experience.

00:31:39.430 --> 00:31:41.450
So we're in a
browser, because it's

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:43.790
modern app for a
modern end-user.

00:31:43.790 --> 00:31:47.510
And look, I'm on the
Wi-Fi inside of Acme Corp.

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:51.080
So we're going to hit
the URL for the HR app.

00:31:51.080 --> 00:31:53.490
Our user is going to
be asked to sign in.

00:31:53.490 --> 00:31:53.990
OK.

00:31:53.990 --> 00:31:55.050
We do that.

00:31:55.050 --> 00:31:56.618
We put in our password.

00:31:56.618 --> 00:31:58.160
And now, because we
have that policy,

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:00.452
we're going to be asked to
authenticate with a security

00:32:00.452 --> 00:32:02.030
key-- again, according
to our policy.

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:03.770
And since we're satisfied
all the requirements--

00:32:03.770 --> 00:32:05.180
we're in the office,
we're authenticated,

00:32:05.180 --> 00:32:07.130
we're authenticated
with the security key--

00:32:07.130 --> 00:32:09.720
we can get in and get
access to that app.

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:12.920
So now let's change things
up a little bit further

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:16.130
and see what happens when we
don't meet the conditions.

00:32:16.130 --> 00:32:17.765
So now we're working remotely.

00:32:17.765 --> 00:32:19.640
You can see that we go
on, and we're actually

00:32:19.640 --> 00:32:22.280
on Starbucks public Wi-Fi
out there on Third Street.

00:32:22.280 --> 00:32:24.890
And so we're going to
go in and [AUDIO OUT]

00:32:24.890 --> 00:32:28.170
for the HR app [AUDIO OUT].

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:29.880
Just like we did before.

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:32.675
Hopefully my mic does not
die while I go through this.

00:32:32.675 --> 00:32:34.800
I only have to talk for
another minute or two here,

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:35.730
so we're hoping.

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:37.772
And we're asked to sign
in with the security key.

00:32:37.772 --> 00:32:39.970
But what happens now?

00:32:39.970 --> 00:32:42.460
The login fails because
we didn't meet all

00:32:42.460 --> 00:32:45.100
the requirements in the policy.

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:46.560
So here-- look.

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:47.980
We have multiple layers.

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:49.120
We have the infrastructure.

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:49.950
We have the app.

00:32:49.950 --> 00:32:50.910
We have the user.

00:32:50.910 --> 00:32:53.520
We have protection that's
working at every level,

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:56.100
working in concert to
provide the security

00:32:56.100 --> 00:33:00.360
and ease of use for the modern
end-user and the way they work.

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:03.820
And one last thing-- we didn't
talk too much about devices,

00:33:03.820 --> 00:33:06.030
although that's one way
you can define policies.

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:09.660
And device trust is a
really, really key input

00:33:09.660 --> 00:33:11.490
for this context-aware
access model.

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:14.990
We know that we can get device
context from Chrome browser

00:33:14.990 --> 00:33:18.810
via an extension, but we know
many organizations already

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:21.120
have mobile management
solutions in place

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:23.670
that they're using to secure
this mobile workforce.

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:26.850
So today, we announced something
we called our BeyondCorp

00:33:26.850 --> 00:33:27.450
Alliance.

00:33:27.450 --> 00:33:31.410
What it is is a partnership with
a number of the key endpoint

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:35.190
and mobile security vendors who
can help provide device status

00:33:35.190 --> 00:33:38.880
data back into Google
Cloud from their products.

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:42.210
So you can use the data that's
coming from these platforms

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:45.330
to make better access
decisions on whether or not

00:33:45.330 --> 00:33:48.360
that product says the device
is in a safe state or not.

00:33:48.360 --> 00:33:50.130
So it's great
validation, I think,

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:52.320
for our vision and
approach of what

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:54.120
we're doing here
from other leaders

00:33:54.120 --> 00:33:57.690
in the end-user
computing security space.

00:33:57.690 --> 00:34:01.440
And that's really a great
segue way into devices.

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:04.310
The final layer we want to
talk about is the device.

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:06.310
And there is a bunch here
we want to go through,

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:08.420
so I'm going to turn
that over to Eugene.

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:10.170
EUGENE LIDERMAN: Really
quickly diving in,

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:11.420
it's so hard to see everybody.

00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:12.880
The light is shining so bright.

00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:14.752
It's like nobody's
actually here.

00:34:14.752 --> 00:34:16.710
So when it comes to
devices, taking a step back

00:34:16.710 --> 00:34:18.967
about devices for a second.

00:34:18.967 --> 00:34:21.300
Obviously, you know Google
has Chrome OS and Android OS,

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:24.150
and we work with a variety
of ecosystem partners, device

00:34:24.150 --> 00:34:24.942
manufacturers.

00:34:24.942 --> 00:34:26.400
Obviously, we have
our own devices,

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:28.710
but there's a huge,
broad ecosystem

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:29.703
of partners out there.

00:34:29.703 --> 00:34:31.620
One of the things that's
really important when

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:35.489
it comes to both
Android OS and Chrome OS

00:34:35.489 --> 00:34:37.860
is Google's commitment to
transparency and openness.

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:40.480
I think Rob touched on
that a little bit earlier.

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:43.469
Obviously, Android
is open-source.

00:34:43.469 --> 00:34:45.750
Chrome OS is built on
Chromium, which is open-source.

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:47.400
Anybody can go see the
contributions that we

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:48.719
make, the changes that we make.

00:34:48.719 --> 00:34:50.909
We blog about new
features as they come out.

00:34:50.909 --> 00:34:53.850
We publish in-depth
white papers and reviews.

00:34:53.850 --> 00:34:56.505
We actually just published an
Android security and privacy

00:34:56.505 --> 00:34:58.930
journal review a
couple weeks back,

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:02.160
and we also publish transparency
reports to highlight

00:35:02.160 --> 00:35:03.490
the state of the ecosystem.

00:35:03.490 --> 00:35:06.160
So we have an Android
ecosystem transparency report.

00:35:06.160 --> 00:35:09.870
We also have a Chrome safe
browsing transparency report.

00:35:09.870 --> 00:35:11.100
It's just part of our ethos.

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:12.767
We try to be transparent
with everything

00:35:12.767 --> 00:35:16.425
we do when it comes to these
mobile operating systems.

00:35:16.425 --> 00:35:18.800
Speaking of security, I think
Rob touched on this before.

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:20.008
Obviously, you hear it a lot.

00:35:20.008 --> 00:35:21.860
It's all about secure by design.

00:35:21.860 --> 00:35:23.790
Security is a
foundational principle.

00:35:23.790 --> 00:35:27.740
We have dedicated security teams
for both Chrome OS Android OS.

00:35:27.740 --> 00:35:30.710
We're continually improving
security, always adding

00:35:30.710 --> 00:35:33.320
additional hardware-based
security features,

00:35:33.320 --> 00:35:38.035
anti-exploitation techniques,
and just implementing it

00:35:38.035 --> 00:35:38.660
at every layer.

00:35:38.660 --> 00:35:40.610
You probably heard that
he said defense in depth,

00:35:40.610 --> 00:35:42.443
layered security, the
onion model-- whatever

00:35:42.443 --> 00:35:43.490
analogy you want to use.

00:35:43.490 --> 00:35:45.470
We're constantly
beefing up our security

00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:47.870
for these operating systems.

00:35:47.870 --> 00:35:54.140
Just to highlight
some of the stack.

00:35:54.140 --> 00:35:58.002
Obviously, like I mentioned,
hardware-based security.

00:35:58.002 --> 00:35:59.460
We obviously used
the Linux kernel.

00:35:59.460 --> 00:36:01.100
So we implement
a lot of security

00:36:01.100 --> 00:36:03.080
around sandboxing app
security, security

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:04.308
for the data on the devices.

00:36:04.308 --> 00:36:06.350
One of the things that's
interesting about having

00:36:06.350 --> 00:36:08.290
a broad ecosystem of
partners is, obviously,

00:36:08.290 --> 00:36:11.040
coming up with design
principles and requirements

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:13.608
and then making sure that
these partners are actually

00:36:13.608 --> 00:36:14.150
implementing.

00:36:14.150 --> 00:36:14.980
Everything's done.

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:17.720
So on the Android side, we have
the compatibility definition

00:36:17.720 --> 00:36:21.080
document that states what must
be implemented by our partners,

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:23.240
and then they go through
various conformance tests

00:36:23.240 --> 00:36:26.750
like CTS, VTS, STS to make
sure that they conform

00:36:26.750 --> 00:36:28.850
and these devices
ship to our standards.

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:33.660
Starting with
hardware-backed security,

00:36:33.660 --> 00:36:36.327
I think Rob mentioned it a
little bit on the GCP side.

00:36:36.327 --> 00:36:38.910
Of course, we want to make sure
that when the device boots up,

00:36:38.910 --> 00:36:41.460
it boots into a verified state
so we have verified boot.

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:43.460
Verified boot, for anybody
that doesn't know it,

00:36:43.460 --> 00:36:46.740
basically verifies the boot-up
process cryptographically

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:47.710
at every stage.

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:50.700
So if something bad happens
during the boot process,

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:54.498
it will alert the user
and react from there.

00:36:54.498 --> 00:36:56.790
Some examples for verified
boot and why it's necessary.

00:36:56.790 --> 00:36:59.947
Of course, obviously
malware is a universal risk.

00:36:59.947 --> 00:37:01.530
The first thing
malware will try to do

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:03.608
is modify the system
partition and persist.

00:37:03.608 --> 00:37:05.400
And so, with verified
boot, when the device

00:37:05.400 --> 00:37:08.460
boots up, if something
bad happens it catches.

00:37:08.460 --> 00:37:09.450
It notifies the user.

00:37:09.450 --> 00:37:11.520
And since these operating
systems are modern,

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:13.440
they actually have
two copies of the OS.

00:37:13.440 --> 00:37:16.295
So what it will do is,
if the primary copy is

00:37:16.295 --> 00:37:17.670
in a known bad
state, it will try

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:20.580
to roll back to the other
copy if it's the same version.

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:27.320
In terms of additional
platform security features that

00:37:27.320 --> 00:37:31.190
are hardware-based,
of course, both OSes

00:37:31.190 --> 00:37:32.990
have full on-device
encryption that

00:37:32.990 --> 00:37:34.532
actually is file-based
encryption, so

00:37:34.532 --> 00:37:36.500
full data arrest.

00:37:36.500 --> 00:37:39.290
They both leverage the
hardware-based security

00:37:39.290 --> 00:37:41.240
for key generation
and key storage.

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:44.910
They both, of course, benefit
from using the Linux kernel.

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:48.135
And so they're both implemented
with mandatory access

00:37:48.135 --> 00:37:49.760
control where everything
is broken down

00:37:49.760 --> 00:37:52.470
in specific sub-components, kind
of protecting one from another,

00:37:52.470 --> 00:37:54.470
making sure that if
something bad does happen it

00:37:54.470 --> 00:37:57.587
doesn't go across the entire OS.

00:37:57.587 --> 00:37:59.420
And then, of course,
as Rob touched earlier,

00:37:59.420 --> 00:38:02.552
with sandboxing, the same thing
happens on the device OSes.

00:38:02.552 --> 00:38:04.010
Of course, we
implement application

00:38:04.010 --> 00:38:07.730
sandboxing for both OSes where
every single app is isolated.

00:38:07.730 --> 00:38:11.300
It has its own resources
such as processing, memory,

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:15.200
and storage, and one cannot go
and eavesdrop on one another.

00:38:15.200 --> 00:38:18.530
And the last thing is, you'll
see over the last couple years

00:38:18.530 --> 00:38:23.960
both OSes have heavily added
additional anti-exploitation

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:25.890
techniques and methods--

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:28.560
so address space
layout randomization

00:38:28.560 --> 00:38:31.610
in both the user space and
the kernel, data execution

00:38:31.610 --> 00:38:34.220
prevention, control
flow integrity.

00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:37.280
The whole mission there is that
the more these techniques we

00:38:37.280 --> 00:38:38.330
can apply--

00:38:38.330 --> 00:38:39.800
obviously, these are large OSes.

00:38:39.800 --> 00:38:41.910
Hundreds of thousands,
millions of lines of code.

00:38:41.910 --> 00:38:44.360
So if we can implement
additional methodologies

00:38:44.360 --> 00:38:47.872
like this, we basically can
help prevent a potential bug

00:38:47.872 --> 00:38:50.330
or vulnerability from becoming
exploitable and compromising

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:51.497
the entire operating system.

00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:56.320
So the next big
thing, and I think

00:38:56.320 --> 00:38:58.270
Rob touched a little
bit on how easy it

00:38:58.270 --> 00:38:59.980
is to update on the cloud side.

00:38:59.980 --> 00:39:02.350
On the mobile device side,
patching is also improved.

00:39:02.350 --> 00:39:04.180
Obviously everybody
knows patch Tuesdays

00:39:04.180 --> 00:39:05.710
and all those good examples.

00:39:05.710 --> 00:39:10.720
On Chrome OS and Android OS,
we provide a nice cadence

00:39:10.720 --> 00:39:11.260
for patches.

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:13.928
For Chrome OS, security patches
come out every three weeks.

00:39:13.928 --> 00:39:15.220
Two to three weeks for Android.

00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:17.530
We provide monthly security
bulletins to the ecosystem.

00:39:20.170 --> 00:39:23.110
The real benefit
there is that we've

00:39:23.110 --> 00:39:26.600
decoupled security updates
from feature updates,

00:39:26.600 --> 00:39:29.800
so they're smaller,
easier to take.

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:31.698
The goal is to make
sure that users

00:39:31.698 --> 00:39:33.490
have an easy way of
adopting these updates,

00:39:33.490 --> 00:39:35.860
and so both OSes levered
seamless updates.

00:39:35.860 --> 00:39:40.450
What that means is, since both
OSes have two copies of the OS

00:39:40.450 --> 00:39:43.320
on the device, what happens
is, when the user is idle

00:39:43.320 --> 00:39:45.820
on the device-- the device is
just idle and not being used--

00:39:45.820 --> 00:39:47.750
the updates download
in the background.

00:39:47.750 --> 00:39:50.240
And when it's ready to
install, it notifies the user.

00:39:50.240 --> 00:39:53.200
So all the user has to do is hit
restart and all of the sudden

00:39:53.200 --> 00:39:56.290
the device reboots and you're
now running the latest update.

00:39:56.290 --> 00:39:59.860
Behind the scenes, what really
happens is as the OS reboots,

00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:02.080
it now switches to
that secondary copy.

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:02.767
It verifies it.

00:40:02.767 --> 00:40:04.600
And if something bad
happens, the nice thing

00:40:04.600 --> 00:40:07.030
is it reverts back to
the last known good copy.

00:40:07.030 --> 00:40:10.810
So there's no risk of
losing data or information.

00:40:10.810 --> 00:40:13.140
On the Android side, obviously
since you walk around

00:40:13.140 --> 00:40:15.307
with your device, it's not
always plugged into power

00:40:15.307 --> 00:40:17.260
and you're using it,
we're a little bit more

00:40:17.260 --> 00:40:18.620
gentle in the way
we do these updates.

00:40:18.620 --> 00:40:20.150
So we obviously wait until
the device is plugged in.

00:40:20.150 --> 00:40:22.360
You're on Wi-Fi, and
it's sitting there idle

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:24.640
so it doesn't impact
your usage of the device.

00:40:27.740 --> 00:40:29.300
Of course, everybody
here is probably

00:40:29.300 --> 00:40:31.670
familiar with the
Google Play Store.

00:40:31.670 --> 00:40:35.450
Google Play Store is what
all Android devices, all GMS

00:40:35.450 --> 00:40:37.370
devices, have for
access to apps.

00:40:37.370 --> 00:40:38.810
That's our central
app repository.

00:40:38.810 --> 00:40:41.270
There's obviously millions
of apps in the Play Store.

00:40:41.270 --> 00:40:43.760
What a lot of people don't
know is that Chrome OS also

00:40:43.760 --> 00:40:46.460
supports Android apps, and
it also has the Play Store.

00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:48.643
And so, for an
enterprise deployment,

00:40:48.643 --> 00:40:50.060
you can actually
deploy these apps

00:40:50.060 --> 00:40:51.768
through what we call
Managed Google Play.

00:40:51.768 --> 00:40:55.610
This is where you can allow IT
to provide a curated experience

00:40:55.610 --> 00:40:57.260
for your applications,
so you can

00:40:57.260 --> 00:40:59.300
decide what applications
you want to deploy.

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:03.115
They have that nice view of just
those approved applications.

00:41:03.115 --> 00:41:05.240
You can push them automatically
down to the device.

00:41:05.240 --> 00:41:06.840
You can control update policies.

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:09.950
You can actually manage
app configurations,

00:41:09.950 --> 00:41:12.260
where if an app
developer supports

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:13.270
various configurations--

00:41:13.270 --> 00:41:15.020
obviously, every
environment is different.

00:41:15.020 --> 00:41:16.853
This allows them to
customize certain things

00:41:16.853 --> 00:41:19.510
and make changes
as they see fit.

00:41:19.510 --> 00:41:22.550
And what's really neat,
especially on the Android side,

00:41:22.550 --> 00:41:25.640
is that since we do
support multiple management

00:41:25.640 --> 00:41:29.180
models with fully managed
and BYOD with a work profile,

00:41:29.180 --> 00:41:32.210
you can actually end up having a
personal instance of Play where

00:41:32.210 --> 00:41:34.970
the user can download
whatever apps they choose.

00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:37.012
And then in the work
profile-- which is basically

00:41:37.012 --> 00:41:38.470
a separate Linux
user on the device

00:41:38.470 --> 00:41:40.290
although it's in the
same user experience--

00:41:40.290 --> 00:41:43.100
you end up having
a separate Managed

00:41:43.100 --> 00:41:44.570
Google Play that IT can curate.

00:41:44.570 --> 00:41:46.070
And there's that
perfect separation

00:41:46.070 --> 00:41:49.580
so that IT's not overreaching
onto the personal side

00:41:49.580 --> 00:41:50.930
for the user and their device.

00:41:55.140 --> 00:41:56.790
Obviously, I think
I touched earlier,

00:41:56.790 --> 00:41:59.940
malware is a universal risk.

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:01.500
Rob touched on
Chrome Safe browsing,

00:42:01.500 --> 00:42:04.943
which is widely used across
Chrome OS and Android.

00:42:04.943 --> 00:42:07.110
The other thing that we
have is Google Play Protect,

00:42:07.110 --> 00:42:09.060
which is our built-in
anti-malware solution.

00:42:09.060 --> 00:42:11.400
It's actually the largest,
or most widely used

00:42:11.400 --> 00:42:14.310
anti-malware solution out there.

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:17.100
It basically scans
over 50 billion apps

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:19.500
across two billion
devices every single day.

00:42:19.500 --> 00:42:22.030
On the back end, it also
analyzes and reanalyzes

00:42:22.030 --> 00:42:24.270
about 500,000 apps
every single day.

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:27.180
It leverages machine learning,
artificial intelligence,

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:29.520
static analysis, dynamic
analysis, heuristics,

00:42:29.520 --> 00:42:33.000
heat maps, and many
other techniques,

00:42:33.000 --> 00:42:36.265
and it works for both off-play
and on-play as well as offline.

00:42:36.265 --> 00:42:37.890
So even if the device
is not connected,

00:42:37.890 --> 00:42:39.397
it's still scanning
and remediating.

00:42:39.397 --> 00:42:41.730
And if it finds a potentially
harmful application, which

00:42:41.730 --> 00:42:44.460
is what we on the Android
security and privacy side

00:42:44.460 --> 00:42:47.550
refer analogous to malware,
it can remediate that

00:42:47.550 --> 00:42:51.493
and remove it off the device or
notify the user, and so forth.

00:42:51.493 --> 00:42:53.910
Like I had mentioned earlier,
we talked about transparency

00:42:53.910 --> 00:42:54.540
and openness.

00:42:54.540 --> 00:42:56.623
We publish an annual year
in review, which we just

00:42:56.623 --> 00:42:57.840
published two weeks ago.

00:42:57.840 --> 00:43:00.420
This year, the likelihood of--

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:02.940
you have a higher likelihood
of dying in a bathtub

00:43:02.940 --> 00:43:05.398
than you do of installing a
potentially harmful application

00:43:05.398 --> 00:43:06.232
from the Play Store.

00:43:06.232 --> 00:43:07.080
I know, very morbid.

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:09.180
Nobody likes that
stat for some reason.

00:43:09.180 --> 00:43:11.727
But I still went with it.

00:43:11.727 --> 00:43:13.560
So this is something
new that we just added.

00:43:13.560 --> 00:43:15.210
We just announced this today.

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:19.620
Again, Rob touched on
hardware security keys.

00:43:19.620 --> 00:43:22.110
Of course, G Suite
supports this.

00:43:22.110 --> 00:43:25.313
A lot of third-party solutions
as well support this.

00:43:25.313 --> 00:43:27.480
As of today, you can actually
use your Android phone

00:43:27.480 --> 00:43:29.340
as a security key.

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:31.680
It's basically Android
Nougat and above.

00:43:31.680 --> 00:43:33.300
That's a billion-plus
devices that

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:35.790
have the ability to now
leverage this functionality.

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:37.925
On all of those
devices, it actually

00:43:37.925 --> 00:43:39.300
does use hardware-based
security,

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:42.240
because it's using the Trusted
Execution Environment to store

00:43:42.240 --> 00:43:43.780
those keys.

00:43:43.780 --> 00:43:49.260
And then, specifically on Pixel
3 devices that have the Titan M

00:43:49.260 --> 00:43:53.340
chip, it actually leverages
that discrete tamper-resistant

00:43:53.340 --> 00:43:55.770
hardware with the strongbox API.

00:43:55.770 --> 00:43:59.190
These, of course, work over
Bluetooth with your existing

00:43:59.190 --> 00:44:00.150
laptops and desktops.

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:06.850
One of the key things
that we focus on

00:44:06.850 --> 00:44:08.290
is measurable security.

00:44:08.290 --> 00:44:09.550
We like to set benchmarks.

00:44:09.550 --> 00:44:10.850
We like to see how we do.

00:44:10.850 --> 00:44:12.430
And so, this is
actually something

00:44:12.430 --> 00:44:15.542
that Gartner
publishes every year.

00:44:15.542 --> 00:44:17.750
They have a new report
actually coming out very soon,

00:44:17.750 --> 00:44:19.042
so this is the previous report.

00:44:19.042 --> 00:44:21.370
But this is the mobile
OS comparison report.

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:22.490
It's pretty comprehensive.

00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:26.020
There's about 28 categories, and
it focuses on platform security

00:44:26.020 --> 00:44:29.800
features like network security,
OS updates, app updates,

00:44:29.800 --> 00:44:32.620
and then specific
enterprise requirements

00:44:32.620 --> 00:44:35.380
around policy management,
authentication controls,

00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:37.100
key management, and so forth.

00:44:37.100 --> 00:44:40.650
And what you'll see highlighted
there in the orange box,

00:44:40.650 --> 00:44:43.510
the ones that scored the
highest in this report

00:44:43.510 --> 00:44:45.100
were all Android devices--

00:44:45.100 --> 00:44:47.908
Android Oreo, the Pixel
2 on Android Oreo,

00:44:47.908 --> 00:44:49.450
and a Samsung device
on Android Oreo.

00:44:49.450 --> 00:44:52.690
In fact, the Pixel 2
received a high rating

00:44:52.690 --> 00:44:55.060
in 24 out of 28 categories,
and comparatively

00:44:55.060 --> 00:45:00.860
if you look at some of the other
OSes, they scored half of that.

00:45:00.860 --> 00:45:04.270
So to close, Google Cloud
with GCP, G Suite, Chrome,

00:45:04.270 --> 00:45:07.322
and Android help you
securely enable--

00:45:07.322 --> 00:45:09.530
this is the last slide that
I never could get right--

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:10.930
securely enable
modern, and these

00:45:10.930 --> 00:45:12.597
are computing with
security capabilities

00:45:12.597 --> 00:45:14.280
from the device to
the data center.

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:17.230
We can help keep your
enterprises secure

00:45:17.230 --> 00:45:18.670
and your users productive.

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:19.180
Thank you.

00:45:19.180 --> 00:45:21.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

